## Answer

**No.** The product functor $X \times - : \mathbf{FSet} \to \mathbf{FSet}$ does **not** have a left adjoint for every finite set $X$. It has a left adjoint only when $X$ is a singleton.

---

## Proof

Suppose $F \dashv (X \times -)$ for some functor $F : \mathbf{FSet} \to \mathbf{FSet}$. Then for all finite sets $Y, Z$ there is a natural bijection

$$\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{FSet}}(F(Y),\, Z) \;\cong\; \mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbf{FSet}}(Y,\, X \times Z). \tag{$\star$}$$

**Counting argument.** Write $|X| = m$, $|Y| = n$, $|Z| = k$, and $|F(Y)| = f$. Since $\mathbf{FSet}$-morphisms are functions,

$$\text{LHS of }(\star):\quad |\mathrm{Hom}(F(Y), Z)| = k^{f}, \qquad \text{RHS of }(\star):\quad |\mathrm{Hom}(Y, X\times Z)| = (mk)^{n} = m^{n}\, k^{n}.$$

Naturality in $Z$ forces these cardinalities to agree for **every** finite $Z$, i.e. for every $k \geq 0$:

$$k^{f} = m^{n}\, k^{n} \quad \text{for all } k \geq 0. \tag{$\dagger$}$$

**Specialize to $k = 1$.** Setting $Z = 1$ (a singleton) gives

$$1 = m^{n}.$$

Hence either $m = 1$ or $n = 0$.

**Case $|X| \geq 2$ (i.e. $m \geq 2$).** Take $Y = 1$ (so $n = 1$). Then $m^{1} = m \geq 2 \neq 1$, contradicting $(\dagger)$ at $k=1$. So no left adjoint exists.

**Case $X = \emptyset$ (i.e. $m = 0$).** Take $Y = 1$ (so $n = 1$). Then $m^{n} = 0^{1} = 0 \neq 1$, again contradicting $(\dagger)$ at $k = 1$. (Equivalently: $X \times Z = \emptyset$ for all $Z$, so $\mathrm{Hom}(Y, \emptyset) = \emptyset$ for $Y \neq \emptyset$, but $\mathrm{Hom}(F(Y), Z)$ always contains at least the maps into $Z = F(Y)$ via the identity—impossible to be empty for all $Z$.) So no left adjoint exists.

**Case $|X| = 1$ (i.e. $m = 1$).** Here $X \times Z \cong Z$ naturally in $Z$, so the functor $X \times -$ is naturally isomorphic to the identity functor $\mathrm{Id}_{\mathbf{FSet}}$. The identity functor is self-adjoint: $\mathrm{Id} \dashv \mathrm{Id}$, witnessed by the identity natural transformation. Concretely, set $F = \mathrm{Id}$; then $(\star)$ becomes $\mathrm{Hom}(Y, Z) \cong \mathrm{Hom}(Y, Z)$, trivially satisfied. So a left adjoint **does** exist (it is $\mathrm{Id}$, up to the natural isomorphism $X \times - \cong \mathrm{Id}$).

---

### Conclusion

The left adjoint to $X \times -$ exists **if and only if** $|X| = 1$. In particular, for $|X| = 0$ and for every $|X| \geq 2$, no left adjoint exists. Therefore the answer to the question is

$$\boxed{\text{No.}}$$

